So they removed the condition for undefined displayCheck. Now, if displayCheck is undefined, the function will skip the display:none check entirely? Actually, the code now only runs the display:none check if displayCheck === 'full'. If displayCheck is undefined, it does nothing and returns false at the end. That should make the tabbable function consider nodes inside a display:none ancestor as tabbable. But wait: the backdrop is no longer display:none when the trap is activated. So why would it still fail?

